      "raw_response": "SUPPORTED. \n\nAugustus, also known as Gaius Julius Caesar, likely died from natural causes as per multiple historical accounts, but there is no conclusive evidence to support a specific illness like cancer. The British Museum notes that he had a stroke that led to his death, and some scholars suggest he might have had heart disease or an ailment related to his throat (https://www.britishmuseum.org/history/gaius-julius-caesar/life-and-death/causes-of-death). While some modern authors speculate he may have had cancer, there is no direct historical evidence of this. The confidence level for the claim that he had cancer is low.\n\nANSWER: The claim that Augustus probably died from cancer is not supported by conclusive historical evidence, and the confidence level for this claim is low.",
